    <abstract>
      <?line 15?>

<t>A profile of RFC 7914's PBKDF2 test vectors for pbkdf2HmacSha256 in
nish/crypto/hmac (std/crypto/hmac.ts). RFC 7914 defines scrypt; its
Section 11 prints test vectors for PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-256, the function scrypt
is built on, and every one of them is held here.</t>
    </abstract>
  </front>
  <middle>
    <?line 22?>

<section anchor="introduction">
      <name>Introduction</name>
      <t>The key words "<bcp14>MUST</bcp14>", "<bcp14>MUST NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>REQUIRED</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHALL</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHALL
NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHOULD</bcp14>", "<bcp14>SHOULD NOT</bcp14>", "<bcp14>RECOMMENDED</bcp14>", "<bcp14>NOT RECOMMENDED</bcp14>",
"<bcp14>MAY</bcp14>", and "<bcp14>OPTIONAL</bcp14>" in this document are to be interpreted as
described in BCP 14 <xref target="RFC2119"/> <xref target="RFC8174"/> when, and only when, they
appear in all capitals, as shown here.</t>
      <?line -18?>

<t>nish/crypto/hmac exports PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA-256 as its pseudorandom
function, over a password held as a <tt>Secret</tt>, a salt, an iteration count
and a derived-key length. SCRAM-SHA-256's <tt>Hi</tt> is this function with a
32-byte output, which is how nish/net/postgres uses it.</t>
      <t>RFC 7914 has no binding sentence in the sense of BCP 14 for PBKDF2, so the
profile does not claim it as its main source: there is no completeness to
check. Section 11 prints each call across two lines, the strings on the first
and <tt>c</tt> and <tt>dkLen</tt> on the second, and vector pairs a printed string only with
values on its own line. Each vector therefore takes its expected value from
Python's hashlib, as a command <tt>spec check --run-sources</tt> runs; the bytes it
prints are the ones Section 11 prints, byte for byte.</t>
    </section>
